              <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
                <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-xs uppercase text-emerald-800 mb-1">1. Tapeworms (Dipylidium caninum)</div>
                <p class="text-xs text-slate-600">
                  Flat, segmented worms contracted by ingesting fleas during grooming. Look for dried, rice-grain-like white segments crawling around your cat's anus or on bedding.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
                <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-xs uppercase text-emerald-800 mb-1">2. Roundworms (Toxocara cati)</div>
                <p class="text-xs text-slate-600">
                  Spaghetti-like cylindrical worms up to 10cm long that live in the small intestine. Causes a classic hard &quot;potbellied&quot; abdomen in kittens, stunted growth, and vomiting.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
                <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-xs uppercase text-emerald-800 mb-1">3. Hookworms (Ancylostoma tubaeforme)</div>
                <p class="text-xs text-slate-600">
                  Microscopic blood-suckers that attach to the intestinal mucosal lining. Causes pale gums, dark tarry black stools (melena), and fatal anemia in young kittens.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
                <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-xs uppercase text-emerald-800 mb-1">4. Giardia &amp; Protozoa</div>
                <p class="text-xs text-slate-600">
